low and  not reliable for selection in addition  we don't really have the machine or  manpower to harvest and way that many  samples so at this stage we're still  working with visual selection and then  those lines move on into the first year  of replicated yield trials it could  potentially be beneficial to have  accurate predictions of green yield at  the f7 small plot stage and that's what  we're trying to look at with aerial  phenotyping so we've kind of designed an  experiment to ask this question can we  use phenotyping to select at the early  generation stage and we design the  experiment so that it would be kind of  integrated into the breeding program and  not use a lot of extra resources so the  breeders went through the f7 small plots  in 2016 to select lines to move on into  the large replicated yield trials in  2017 we went through and selected a  subset of those 1080 lines and planted  them again it's unreplicated one meter  small plots so these line these lines  were planted twice in 2016 both small  plots and as large yield plots we used  to mitrice 100 UAS by DJI equipped with  a micro sense red edge multispectral  sensor to phenotype the plots at  multiple time points during the growing  season we also assessed yield in both  the small and large plots again we don't  normally assess yield and small plot so  we did so this year for the purpose of  this experiment I want to take you guys  through some of the steps of the aerial  phenotyping and imaging pipeline that we  use at CIMMYT because there are a lot of  considerations that we have to get right  in order to get usable phenotype out at  the end before we can even fly there are  a few things to do and one of those  things is to make and place ground  control points in the field these need  to stay in one place throughout the  growing season and they need to be  visible to the sensor once we've  collected their RTK GPS coordinates with  our RTK system we can use that  information to  signed physical locations to each of the  images which is critical to being able  to stitch them together we also need to  create a flight plan for the UAS and we  do that in a software called mission  planner again in order to stitch the  images they have to have sufficient  overlap so we shoot for 80% overlap and  75% side lap and we can program that  into the flight plan the altitude speed  of the UAS and capture rate of the  sensor can also affect the overall  quality of the image and we fly the mic  ascents at 30 meters at 12 km/h and the  sensor has a capture rate of one image  per second on the day of the flight  there's a lot of things to remember to  bring out to the field batteries are  actually a big bottleneck for us we  bring out multiple SD cards for the  images and a computer to store them on  at the end um we run an app on a tablet  that will control the UAV and it runs  the flight plan mostly automatically so  this very little manual control of the  UAS that's needed before every takeoff  we also take images of a calibration  panel calibrating the images is really  important because atmospheric conditions  on the day of the flight can affect the  amount of light that's captured by the  sensor so what we do is we measure the  true values of the calibration panel  with a spectral radiometer and then  compare those two values taken on the  day of the flight and create a model  that will calibrate all of the images  prior to stitching this is really  important if you want to compare  phenotypes on different dates and track  grow throughout the growing season we  stitch all the images together using a  software called pix for D and the  composite image that comes out is called  an ortho mosaic I put up our ortho  mosaic of the small plots area from one  time point and I've taken out a section  for you to see up close what it looks  like that little white thing in there is  a ground control point our final  resolution is one to two centimeters for  pixels so it's pretty good again these  are the 1 meter plots so they're fairly  small  to extract the data we use our GIS and  first thing we a soil vegetation mask  using a maximum likelihood  classification so this will sign a  binary value to pixels that are either  soil or vegetation we can apply that  mask back to the original image and  remove any data associated with soil and  then the last step is to draw polygons  around the plots and calculate plot  level statistics on the data bound by  the polygon so there are a lot of  considerations that go into this and  hopefully if you do it right you can get  a good quality results and that's what  we think we're starting to see here in  both the small and the large plots we  had higher heritability for all of our  MB bi time points than we did for yields  so that's promising in our in terms of  yield we had a correlation of 0.3  between the small and large plots that's  about what we expected and in both  experiments we saw significant  correlations between NDVI and yield it  was a little bit greater in the large  plots due to their size the relationship  that we're really interested in however  is between small plot NDVI and large  flat yield and here we're seeing that  it's 0.4 3 so that's pretty good I  looked at this in terms of correlated  response to selection so in this case  the correlated trait is actually large  flat yield and we could theoretically  select off of small plot you small flat  nd bi or small pot yield and we're  seeing that the NDVI is twice as  effective the reason for this is because  it has higher heritability and it has a  higher correlation with large pot yields  than small pot yield so these are  looking promising as a selection tool I  also did some multi trait genomic  prediction work and this is work sort of  following Jessica's initial work using  hgp traits as secondary traits in gs and  the set up that I use is a little bit  complicated so I wanted to go through it  the training set that I used was 80% of  the lines the target trait was large  flat yield and the secondary trait was  large plot NDVI so we fit this model  using pedigrees and then for the text  that we use the remaining 20% of lines  yield was hidden  this time and small-plot NDVI was  actually the second the secondary trade  so the predictions that we got out we  correlated back to large flat yield so  the idea of doing this is you could fit  a model using the large plots where you  have both yield and NDVI available and  then use it to predict the small plots  where you only have an ABI and assess  what their yield would be as if they  were planted as large plots so I've  plotted the results along the bottom in  white and we're looking at multiple time  points for the NDVI so I tried to show  where those are in relation to the  phenology of the trials that's what the  top two graphs are um CIMMYT has a huge  aerial phenotyping program I think we  did more aerial phenotyping last season  than any CG Center has ever done we have  a lot of different experiments going on  so unfortunately those things as well as  changes in the weather and  responsibilities in the field prevented  us from phenotyping the both trials in  the same date we also had a lot more  phenotyping dates that we had assessed  but several of them didn't make it  through our quality control pipeline so  these are the dates that we have we have  two in the small pots and four in the  large plots you can see in the results  that adding NDB is the secondary rate  doesn't improve over the univariate case  in most cases but it does so twice most  notably when both the small and large  plots for phenotyped on march 13th we  know that NDB is sensitive to phenology  so this coming season we will be paying  a lot more attention to trying to  phenotype these trials at the same time  we got them planted on the exact same  date this year so that's really good we  just want to make sure that we're doing  our best so that we're not comparing  apples oranges one more thing I want to  say about this is in this experiment the  training set and the test set were  assessed within the same breeding cycle  but if we were to do this in practice  the training and test sets would be one  to two breeding cycles removed so it's  possible that what we're seeing here is  a little bit of within cycle  cross-validation bias which is  characterized in the  literature and that NDVI could actually  be more beneficial than what we're  seeing but we have not assessed that yet  so some quick take-home messages the  small plot Arial NDVI traits tend to be  more heritable than yield they are  predictive of yield in the large plots  and they're correlated response is twice  as effective as the small plot yield  with our multi trait GS and EB I does  have the potential to improve accuracy  about timing around the phenology  appears to be really important and we  will be taking steps to improve that  this coming year so that's what I have  for you guys today and I'd like to thank  my committee especially my advisers mark  and Mike at CIMMYT I'm working with dr.  Jose Costa who's in biometrics but as  you can see from the photos there are a  lot of other people who helped make this  phenotyping possible this project has  also been heavily supported by Kansas  State jesse has been Co advising the  project his UAS technician rich brown  who's pictured there taught me how to  fly and attina has been helping with  some of the image processing and of  course I wouldn't be in Mexico without  my funding sources so thank you to USAID  and their us for lab dollars in global  food security grant as well as the  National Science Foundation grant so I'm  happy to interact with you guys for a  couple minutes if we still have time  [Applause]  questions yeah or I could run the mic to  ya tier so don't obrigada  you know when you're taking NDVI images  is the lack of greenness and that kind  of you know being robust for it's not  robust healthy versus not healthy is  that drought heat nitrogen stress all  three of them other things I mean like  what fundamentally is the like yeah I  guess what is it stress tolerance or  stress resistance or whatnot that is  being reflected in NDVI what are you  selecting on like terms of that  yeah so in terms of that for this  particular experiment it's probably just  gonna be a lot to do with biomass in the  small pots you know germination things  like that but we are using these  technologies at CIMMYT in our other  environments and so we have a drought  experiment we have a heat experiment and  in those experiments we see a lot of  difference a lot more variation in NDVI  that would be linked to those things  Margaret this dan could you pull back a  couple slides to the prediction  accuracies on yeah so the on march 13th  do you think the higher prediction  accuracy there is an artifact of being  scored on the same day and large and  small plots because it's just kind of an  arbitrary time in Granville or do you  think that's actually legitimate is that  the time points that tends to be the  most predictive in our program tend to  be during grain filling and we haven't  necessarily focused all of our  phenotyping efforts to just focus on the  grain fill stage because I think the  breeders are interested in having  phenotypes from earlier in the season  certainly it takes time to process the  imagery so to have phenotypes in the  hands of the breeders at the time that  they're going through selections you  might have to start earlier so um that's  something that it'll be interesting to  see what the results come out as next  season if it's only grain filling that  is the time when we can phenotype  effectively well if there's no more  questions let's all thank Margaret  [Applause]  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu

